The consensus among climate scientists is supported by extensive peer-reviewed research and data from multiple scientific institutions worldwide.
The IPCC, which includes thousands of scientists from around the globe, has consistently stated that human-induced climate change is the most significant threat to global stability.
The observed rise in global temperatures, extreme weather events, and sea-level rise align with the predictions of climate models that incorporate human activities as a primary factor.
The overwhelming agreement among scientists is similar to the consensus in other scientific fields, such as medicine or physics, where evidence-based conclusions are widely accepted.
Ignoring the consensus on climate change could lead to irreversible environmental damage, affecting future generations and global economic stability.